# Usage notes Grandpa Park explains (languages/ko/notes.json). note-<id>-title and note-<id>.

note-yo-title = Ending in 요
note-yo = Polite Korean sentences end in 요 (yo): 있어요, "there is"; 몰라요, "I don't know". To strangers, shopkeepers and your landlady, keep the 요. Drop it only with close friends. The same verb asks or answers: 배고파요? "Hungry?", 배고파요. "I'm hungry."

note-ieyo-title = 이에요 and 예요
note-ieyo = "Is" after a noun is 이에요 when the noun ends in a consonant (방이에요, "it's a room") and 예요 after a vowel (친구예요, "it's a friend"). Introducing yourself, 입니다 works after anything: 저는 …입니다, "I am …".

note-numbers-title = Two sets of numbers
note-numbers = Korean counts things with its own numbers: 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯. Before a counter they shorten: 한 개, 두 개, 세 개, 네 개, "one, two, three, four (things)". Prices, months and money use the other set, borrowed from Chinese: 오 (five), 삼 (three), 천 (thousand), 만 (ten thousand). So 오천 원 is 5,000 won, and 삼월 is March.

note-neun-i-title = 은/는 and 이/가
note-neun-i = 는 (after a vowel) or 은 (after a consonant) marks what you're talking about: 저는 …, "as for me …". 이 (after a consonant) or 가 (after a vowel) marks the subject, often something new or asked about: 이름이 뭐예요? "What's your name?" When in doubt: 는 for "as for", 이/가 for the question.
